# ---- Simulation output ----
# Every Verilator build directory. One per module, named obj_<module> by the
# Makefile, so the pattern must be a wildcard — plain `obj_dir/` matches only
# Verilator's default and silently misses obj_i960_dec/ and every sibling.
obj_*/
*.o
*.d
*.a
*.vcd
*.fst
*.wlf
transcript
work/
verilator.log

# ---- Editor / OS ----
*.swp
*~
.DS_Store
.idea/

# Editor config IS tracked — it carries the reasoning for why six nested git
# repositories are hidden from Source Control. Everything else under .vscode is
# personal state.
.vscode/*
!.vscode/settings.json

# Quartus spike output
build/

# MAME output. It writes these into the working directory on every run.
#
# `nvram/` is the reason this section is not merely tidiness: it holds EEPROM
# and battery-backed RAM contents dumped out of the game, which is ROM-DERIVED
# DATA and must never enter this repository. `cfg/` and the rest are machine
# state and personal settings that would be noise even if they were harmless.
#
# Runs should also pass -cfg_directory/-nvram_directory outside the tree, per
# tools/m2b-mame.sh. This section is the safety net, not the mechanism.
cfg/
nvram/
sta/
snap/
diff/
inp/
memcard/
comment/
ui/
mame.ini
ui.ini
error.log
debug.log
*.tr
build_id.v

# Python bytecode, created whenever a tool here is imported rather than run
__pycache__/

# Quartus writes this next to the project file on every compile
cr_ie_info.json
# tools/fit-numbers.sh archives (it now defaults under build/, this is the net)
fit-*.blocks
fit-*.summary

# ---- releases/ IS NOT PUBLISHED YET ----
#
# The folder is staged locally in the layout MiSTer expects -- one dated .rbf,
# the MRAs, and a README -- but nothing in it is committed, because the core is
# not ready to be released: Daytona boots and has sound, and draws no 3D.
#
# WHEN IT IS READY, the way to publish it is to replace this block with
# negations that MUST COME LAST in this file, because gitignore takes the last
# matching rule and there is a blanket `*.rbf` above for Quartus output:
#
#     !/releases/
#     !/releases/*.rbf
#
# Without them `git add releases/` succeeds, stages NOTHING, and the push lands
# upstream with a README promising a core that is not there. The Kaneko16
# project shipped exactly that and recorded it.
/releases/
